config MESHTASTIC_THREAD_SUMMARY
	bool "Periodic condensed per-thread stack/CPU summary"
	default y
	depends on THREAD_ANALYZER
	help
	  Own periodic thread that calls the same public thread_analyzer_run()
	  API upstream's CONFIG_THREAD_ANALYZER_AUTO uses, but with a condensed
	  callback: batches 3 threads per LOG_INF line ("name stack%/cpu%"
	  triplets) instead of upstream's own two-lines-per-thread format
	  (a "STACK: ..." line and a separate "Total CPU cycles used: ..."
	  line for every thread).

	  Exists because CONFIG_THREAD_ANALYZER_AUTO's built-in dump -- left
	  as CONFIG_THREAD_ANALYZER_AUTO=y in prj.conf until 2026-08-09 -- is
	  genuinely large: ~29 threads on this build (see `kernel thread
	  stacks`) x 2 lines = ~58 near-simultaneous LOG_INF calls every
	  interval, which regularly overran the deferred-log ring buffer
	  ("--- N messages dropped ---" in the console/netlog output). This
	  reports the same two numbers that actually flag a problem at a
	  glance -- a climbing stack% signals encroaching overflow risk (the
	  same class of near-miss STABILITY-2, 2026-08-05, caught); a stuck-
	  high cpu% signals a runaway or starved thread -- and drops the
	  absolute cycles-used counter, which isn't actionable without a
	  second reading to diff against anyway.

	  Mutually exclusive with CONFIG_THREAD_ANALYZER_AUTO in spirit (both
	  can technically be on at once, but that just doubles the reporting
	  this exists to reduce) -- prj.conf leaves AUTO off by default now.

if MESHTASTIC_THREAD_SUMMARY

config MESHTASTIC_THREAD_SUMMARY_INTERVAL
	int "Interval between summaries (seconds)"
	default 300
	help
	  Matches the interval CONFIG_THREAD_ANALYZER_AUTO_INTERVAL used to be
	  set to here (5 min) -- enough to catch a trend without spamming.

config MESHTASTIC_THREAD_SUMMARY_STACK_SIZE
	int "Stack size for the summary thread"
	default 2048
	help
	  Matches the stack size CONFIG_THREAD_ANALYZER_AUTO_STACK_SIZE used
	  to be set to here -- this thread walks and formats every other
	  thread's info, doubled from the Zephyr default during an earlier
	  soak that measured this exact job as the tightest margin on the
	  bench (STABILITY-2 investigation, 2026-08-05).

endif # MESHTASTIC_THREAD_SUMMARY
